{
  "id": "80790",
  "type": "Page",
  "tags": [],
  "title": "Brønsted-Lowry Acids and Bases",
  "content": {
    "bibrefs": [],
    "model": [
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "In this unit, we look at the characteristics of and interactions between "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "acids"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "Substances that produce H"
                      },
                      {
                        "sub": true,
                        "text": "3"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": "O"
                      },
                      {
                        "sup": true,
                        "text": "+"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": " when dissolved in water."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "1463009558",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "3203598311",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": " and "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "bases"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "Substances that produce OH"
                      },
                      {
                        "sup": true,
                        "text": "−"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": " when dissolved in water."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "1361218640",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "3545449910",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": ". When Robert Boyle characterized acids and bases in 1680, he noted that acids dissolve many substances, change the color of certain natural dyes (e.g., they change litmus from blue to red), and lose these characteristic properties after coming into contact with alkalis (bases). In the 18th century, acids were further described: they have a sour taste, react with limestone to liberate a gaseous substance (now known to be CO"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "), and react with most metals to produce hydrogen gas. In 1815, Humphry Davy contributed greatly to the development of the modern acid–base concept by demonstrating that hydrogen is the essential constituent of acids. Around that same time, Joseph Louis Gay-Lussac concluded that acids are substances that can neutralize bases and that these two classes of substances can be defined only in terms of each other. The significance of hydrogen was reemphasized in 1884 when Svante Arrhenius defined an acid as a compound that dissolves in water to yield hydrogen cations, H"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "+"
              },
              {
                "text": " (now recognized to be hydronium ions, H"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "text": "O"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "+"
              },
              {
                "text": ") and a base as a compound that dissolves in water to yield hydroxide anions, OH"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "−"
              },
              {
                "text": "."
              }
            ],
            "id": "dn0r9q5rxlekceg",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Arrhenius's definitions of "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "acid"
              },
              {
                "text": " and "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "base"
              },
              {
                "text": " work well much of the time but are limited because they apply only to aqueous solutions. We now extend the definitions using the more general approach proposed in 1923 by the Danish chemist Johannes Brønsted and the English chemist Thomas Lowry. These definitions focus on the "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "proton"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "A positively charged subatomic particle located in the nucleus of the atom."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "616194499",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "2017723092",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": " H"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "+"
              },
              {
                "text": ". A proton is what remains when the most common isotope of hydrogen, "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": ""
                  }
                ],
                "legacyBlockRendered": false,
                "src": "{}_1^1\\mathrm H",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula_inline"
              },
              {
                "text": ", loses an electron. A "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "compound"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "A pure substance that can be broken down into simpler substances by chemical changes."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "3281370100",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "3658553407",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": " that donates a proton to another compound is called a "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "Brønsted-Lowry acid"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "A substance that donates a proton."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "2973541071",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "3830826216",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": ", and a compound that accepts a proton is called a "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "Brønsted-Lowry base"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "A substance that accepts a proton."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "2528941284",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "979598022",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": ". An acid–base reaction is therefore the transfer of a proton from a proton donor (acid) to a proton acceptor (base). Later in this course, we introduce the most general model of acid–base behavior introduced by the American chemist G. N. Lewis."
              }
            ],
            "id": "9v0pupl9i02brup",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Acids may be compounds such as HCl or H"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "SO"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "4"
              },
              {
                "text": ", organic acids such as acetic acid (CH"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "text": "COOH) or ascorbic acid (vitamin C), or H"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "O. "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "Anions"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": " A negatively charged ion. "
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "2839969337",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "1146956993",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": " (e.g., HSO"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "4"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "−"
              },
              {
                "text": ", H"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "PO"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "4"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "−"
              },
              {
                "text": ", HS"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "−"
              },
              {
                "text": ", and HCO"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "−"
              },
              {
                "text": ") and "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "cations"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "A positively charged ion."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "1707295441",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "1815553715",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": " (e.g., H"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "text": "O"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "+"
              },
              {
                "text": ", NH"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "4"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "+"
              },
              {
                "text": ", and [Al(H"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "O)"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "6"
              },
              {
                "text": "]"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "3+"
              },
              {
                "text": ") may also act as acids. Bases fall into the same three categories. Bases may be neutral molecules (e.g., H"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "O, NH"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "text": ", and CH"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "text": "NH"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "), anions (e.g., OH"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "−"
              },
              {
                "text": ", HS"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "−"
              },
              {
                "text": ", HCO"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "−"
              },
              {
                "text": ", CO"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "2−"
              },
              {
                "text": ", F"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "−"
              },
              {
                "text": ", and PO"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "4"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "3−"
              },
              {
                "text": "), or cations (e.g., [Al(H"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "O)"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "5"
              },
              {
                "text": "OH]"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "2+"
              },
              {
                "text": "). The most familiar bases are ionic compounds such as NaOH and Ca(OH)"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": ", which contain the hydroxide "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "ion"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "An atom that has gained or lost electrons and therefore is electrically charged."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "3552351343",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "3909113061",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": " OH"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "−"
              },
              {
                "text": ". The hydroxide ion in these compounds accepts a proton from acids to form water:"
              }
            ],
            "id": "8jtkg7ikjdpn0tk",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "tlod8kwab3t8ng7",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "pn0rhkymcoxlnqq",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "\\mathrm H^+\\;+\\;\\mathrm{OH}^-\\;\\rightarrow\\;{\\mathrm H}_2\\mathrm O",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": ""
              }
            ],
            "id": "1299432034",
            "type": "p"
          }
        ],
